// Only aggregates may be `mutable`, and that is a rule about MEANING rather than a limitation.
//
// A scalar is copied when it crosses, so `mutable n: Int` could only mean "you get your own copy to
// change" — a fact about the body, not about the call. One word would then mean two different things
// depending on the type, decided silently, which is precisely what this language is arranged against.
// A local copy is written where the copy is: `let mutable own: Int = n;`.
function counted(mutable n: Int) -> Int {
    n = n + 1;
    return n;
}
print(counted(1));
